Working with the cart module

The cart module stores every selected product in a plain object, and the checkout page reads from that object when it renders the order summary. Before you change anything, run the test suite once and note which cases already pass, because the pricing rules are easy to break and the failures are not always obvious. The example below creates a cart, adds three products, and then walks the object to print a short summary. Keys are product codes rather than display names, since the pricing service expects codes and returns names separately. When a code is missing from the catalogue, the service answers with a placeholder and the page shows a warning banner above the total.

The helper that builds the cart lives in cart.js, and the tests in cart.test.js mock the pricing service so that no network call is made. Notice how the loop below avoids sorting the keys: the order does not matter for a summary, and skipping the sort keeps the function cheap on large carts. If you need a stable order, sort the keys once and reuse the array. The exported names at the bottom of the file are the only public entry points; everything else is private to the module.

During development you will often want to see the contents of a cart after a sequence of operations, and the simplest tool is a loop that prints each entry together with its quantity. The console output is not pretty, but it is fast and it does not require a debugger. When a product code appears twice, the add helper merges the quantities rather than creating a second entry, so the summary always shows one line per product. If you only need the total, call the total helper directly instead of walking the object yourself.

Remove the entry when the quantity drops to zero, otherwise a stale key will survive the next recalculation and the totals will drift. The drift is subtle: the badge on the cart icon counts keys, not quantities, so an empty entry makes the badge show one more item than the shopper expects. Tests for this behaviour live in the merge section of the suite and run on every commit. A nightly job prunes empty carts from the database, so the badge issue usually disappears on its own.

The checkout button is disabled while the totals are being recalculated. A spinner replaces the label, and a short message below the button explains that the order is being priced. If the request fails, the button returns to its normal state and the message changes to an apology with a retry link. The retry link calls the same pricing helper with the last request, and it logs the failure to the console so that support can find it later. Do not cache the totals between pages, because a discount code can change them at any moment and a stale total is worse than a slow one.

